{"kind":"promise","text":"I will review the addressed ephemeral inbox runtime at exact head c0c70300381730f3952db3c0a837c2beeb04121c, against all five artifacts, and edit nothing.\n\nThis is a delivery system with a recipient field, so I will treat the addressing as the security surface rather than as a feature. The questions I will actually try to answer: can a frame be delivered to someone it was not addressed to, can a recipient be forged or a reply attributed to the wrong actor, can a message be lost between enqueue and acknowledgement, and can a full inbox lose a frame it had already accepted rather than refusing a new one. Where a claim is about behaviour under a boundary — an old resident, a restart, a sessionless browser — I will exercise it rather than read it.\n\nThe legacy compatibility claim I will test rather than trust. A mention-token precheck that keeps email addresses and file paths from resolving is the sort of thing that works on the examples in the tests and fails on the one in production, so I will look for the boundary cases: an address at the start of a line, a path that contains a name, punctuation adjacent to a token.\n\nI will run proportionate gates rather than the whole suite for its own sake: the packages this touches, race where concurrency is claimed, the UI checks, build and diff-check.\n\nI will report approved or changes-requested with body.head exactly this commit and body.artifact naming the cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact, resting on all five — which is the shape that failed to resolve when we did it the other way round.","body":{"head":"c0c70300381730f3952db3c0a837c2beeb04121c"}}